=[ sisho ]

Generated by `sisho spec cli` from the clap definitions in `app/src/cli.rs`. Do not edit it: change the doc comments there and run the command again.

Orchestrate the Tomet ecosystem (tomet, twrit, tandr)

```text
sisho [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]
```

Options:

- `-j, --jobs <N>`: Number of parallel jobs to run. Only matters for the commands that run tools in parallel (`check`, `fix`). If a jobserver was inherited from the environment, it wins and `-j` is not used.
- `-l, --list`: List installed extensions found on PATH.
- `--all`: Show all matching executables on PATH, including hidden duplicates.
- `-v, --verbose`: Show metadata reported by extensions.

==[ sisho check ]

Verify a project without writing anything: run every tool's checks over PATH and report which passed.

The steps run at the same time and are reported in plan order. One failing step does not stop the others; the run exits 1 if any step failed, and a tool that is not installed counts as a failure. Exit code 2 means `sisho` itself could not do its job.

```text
sisho check [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: File or directory to run over (defaults to ".").

Options:

- `--json`: Print the result as JSON instead of human-readable text.
- `-v, --verbose`: Show the output of steps that passed too, not only of those that failed. Conflicts with `--json`.
- `-j, --jobs <N>`: Number of parallel jobs to run. Only matters for the commands that run tools in parallel (`check`, `fix`). If a jobserver was inherited from the environment, it wins and `-j` is not used.

==[ sisho fix ]

Repair what the tools can repair in a project.

The steps write files, so they run one after another in a fixed order, each reported as soon as it ends. One failing step does not stop the others. Run `sisho check` afterwards to confirm.

```text
sisho fix [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: File or directory to run over (defaults to ".").

Options:

- `--json`: Print the result as JSON instead of human-readable text.
- `-v, --verbose`: Show the output of steps that passed too, not only of those that failed. Conflicts with `--json`.
- `-j, --jobs <N>`: Number of parallel jobs to run. Only matters for the commands that run tools in parallel (`check`, `fix`). If a jobserver was inherited from the environment, it wins and `-j` is not used.

==[ sisho check-links ]

Check every `@file`, `@dir`, `@embed` and `@link` in a document, or in every document under a directory, for a local target that does not exist.

What it learns about a document is kept in an SQLite cache in the user's cache directory (`~/.cache/tomet/` on Unix), never inside the vault, so checking a large vault again does not parse what has not changed. Exits 1 if a link is broken or a document could not be read.

```text
sisho check-links [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: File or directory to check (defaults to ".").

Options:

- `--json`: Print the report as JSON instead of human-readable text.
- `-j, --jobs <N>`: Number of parallel jobs to run. Only matters for the commands that run tools in parallel (`check`, `fix`). If a jobserver was inherited from the environment, it wins and `-j` is not used.

==[ sisho convert ]

Change a document, or a directory of them, from one format to another.

`--to` reads Tomet and writes commonmark, html, typst or pandoc (its JSON AST, which `pandoc -f json` turns into every format Pandoc writes). `--from` reads md or pandoc (`pandoc -t json x.docx | sisho convert - --from pandoc`) and writes Tomet. The input is a file, `-` for standard input, or a directory; the output goes to standard output, to `--out`, or next to the source with `--in-place`. A directory is converted into files, so it needs `--out`, `--in-place` or `--dry-run`.

Nothing is read from a document's own `@config`: that is `sisho export`. This is the conversion you ask for by name.

```text
sisho convert [OPTIONS] <PATH>
```

Arguments:

- `<PATH>`: File or directory to convert, or `-` for standard input.

Options:

- `--to <FORMAT>`: Convert from Tomet to this format: commonmark (md), html, typst, pandoc. Conflicts with `--from`.
- `--from <FORMAT>`: Convert to Tomet from this format: md (commonmark), pandoc.
- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write here instead of standard output: a file, or a directory (for a file, it gets one named after the source; for a directory, it is written into, keeping the sub-directories).
- `-i, --in-place`: Write next to each source, with the new extension. Conflicts with `--out`.
- `--remove-original`: Delete each source once its result is written. Only with `--from`: converting to Tomet is a migration, and a Tomet source is not something to throw away. Needs `--out` or `--in-place`.
- `--dry-run`: Convert and check, and write nothing.
- `--advanced`: With `--to html`: number headings by nesting level and give them slugs.
- `--lang <LANG>`: With `--to html`: `<html lang="...">` for the page shell.
- `--body`: With `--to html`: just the rendered body, with no `<html>` / `<head>` shell, for templating into a page of your own. `--lang` has no effect alongside this.
- `-j, --jobs <N>`: Number of parallel jobs to run. Only matters for the commands that run tools in parallel (`check`, `fix`). If a jobserver was inherited from the environment, it wins and `-j` is not used.

==[ sisho serve ]

Serve a document as HTML over HTTP on 127.0.0.1, rendering it again on every request: edit the file and reload the page.

It is the rendering of `sisho convert --to html`. A document that cannot be rendered is shown as an error page with status 500, so the browser tells you what is wrong. Stop it with Ctrl+C.

```text
sisho serve [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`: The document to serve.

Options:

- `-p, --port <PORT>`: The port to listen on; 0 takes a free one. Default: `8787`.
- `--advanced`: Number headings by nesting level and give them slugs.
- `--lang <LANG>`: `<html lang="...">` for the page shell. Defaults to "ja".
- `-j, --jobs <N>`: Number of parallel jobs to run. Only matters for the commands that run tools in parallel (`check`, `fix`). If a jobserver was inherited from the environment, it wins and `-j` is not used.

==[ sisho spec ]

Generate the CLI reference specification in `.tmt` format from the clap definitions.

Prints to standard output when `--out` is omitted. With `--out`, writes `cli.tmt` into the given directory (or to the named file). With `--check`, exits 1 if the file on disk disagrees with the generated specification, without writing anything.

```text
sisho spec [OPTIONS] [TARGET]
```

Arguments:

- `[TARGET]`: Documentation target to generate (`cli`).

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this file or directory instead of standard output.
- `--check`: Fail if the output file is missing or out of date, without writing.
- `-j, --jobs <N>`: Number of parallel jobs to run. Only matters for the commands that run tools in parallel (`check`, `fix`). If a jobserver was inherited from the environment, it wins and `-j` is not used.
